PARAGRAPHS WITHIN EACH STREAM
Use grammar and discourse to decide paragraph connection within the chosen stream, with indentation and spacing as supporting evidence. A paragraph may contain several complete sentences. An outgoing wrap to the next page does not establish an incoming join to an earlier paragraph.

printed_lines reports raster observations, not semantic verdicts. median_ink_height is measured ink height; Compare an unnumbered tail with both main prose and preceding notes using these observations and literal seams, never fixed size thresholds. A first_line_extra_indent_vs_later_lines_in_same_box is RELATIVE TO LATER LINES IN THE SAME BOX: whole-quotation or column indentation is already subtracted. A positive value can establish a new paragraph INSIDE a note, not body ownership. The inset value is measured in median printed line heights, not pixels. first_line_indent_confidence is a 0–1 heuristic confidence that the first line has extra indentation relative to later lines in that box. It summarizes measured displacement and alignment agreement; it is not a calibrated probability or a paragraph/join decision. OCR boxes, noise and unusual layouts can make it misleading. Use it only as optional evidence alongside your own grammatical, discourse and layout judgment; you remain free to disagree. A null score means insufficient measurements, not evidence of no indentation. Missing observations are unknown. printed_lines.left_offsets are local observed line starts; a multi-line bbox's left edge is not necessarily its first-line start. Source geometry does not change when slicing, and observations for the original first line cannot be invented for later slices.

